I built and saved the workflow for slot w3-rB2 and it starts. One chat turn returned the correct opening line: Freya Bank's digital assistant, call recorded, "Am I speaking with Ms Lee?". The lint found 0 errors and the situations check found no problems. I ran no practice cases and no longer chat tests, and I skipped the rule-by-rule re-read of the brief because of the time limit.

The call flow is: the given date-facts code node (unchanged) → opening → identity → router → negotiate. Each exit situation has its own specialist node, and every close goes to the `hang_up` end node. The router calls `get_account` itself, using your `account_post_process_code` verbatim as its hook. The routing recipe asks for a separate passthrough lookup node instead, which I left out to save time. `{{date_facts}}` and the "never compute a date yourself" instruction are in router, negotiate, hardship and callback. The three rules no code step computes are written into the negotiate node in the brief's words, along with "offer exactly the `offer_instead` value".

The generator script is `build_w3/gen_rB2.py`; it writes `out/w3-rB2.workflow.json`.

I changed the extracted situations in three places because brief B overrides them:
- **`already_paid` and `dispute` do not close the call.** Brief B clause 9 says a paid claim or dispute on one product doesn't end the call. These nodes write an `add_note` for that product (reason paid or dispute) and go back to negotiate for the remaining products.
- **`refused` notes one product and returns to negotiate.** It only closes once every product has a promise or a note, using the clause 12 closing order.
- **"Refused after all persuasion attempts" goes to `refused`, not `cannot_pay_transfer`.** Brief A says that case closes as `no_promise` / `refused` with no transfer, so `cannot_pay_transfer` now only handles a payment-plan request (restructuring queue) or an insisted human request (general queue).

From both memos, the deadlines are 12 business days (early band) and 7 (late band). Up to 3 products are handled. A failed `create_promise` is tried twice per product, then noted as `service_error`. No notes are written on any transfer.

